==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|kneo|lang=goh}}, from {{proto|Germanic|knewan|lang=de}} (compare Low German {{term|Knee|lang=nds}}, Dutch {{term|knie|lang=nl}}, Swedish {{term|knä|lang=sv}}), from {{proto|Indo-European|ǵnéwo-|lang=de}}, a thematic derivative of {{proto|Indo-European|ǵónu-|lang=de}}. Compare {{etyl|hit|-}} {{term|𒄀𒉡|tr=genu|lang=hit|sc=Xsux}}, Latin {{term|genu|genū|lang=la}}, {{etyl|xto|-}} {{term|kanweṃ|lang=xto}} (dual), {{etyl|txb|-}} {{term|kenī|lang=txb}}, Ancient Greek {{term|γόνυ||knee|tr=góny|lang=grc}}, {{term|γωνία||corner, angle|tr=gōnía|lang=grc}}, Old Armenian {{term|ծունր||tr=cunr|lang=xcl}}, Avestan {{term|𐬲𐬥𐬎𐬨||tr=žnum|lang=ae|sc=Avst}}, Sanskrit {{term|जानु|tr=jānu|lang=sa}}.

